# README — REQ-011

## Scope

REQ-011 implements the **Task Inbox and State Badges** frontend screen for the Zen 2030 document management platform. This is a Next.js 14 page providing:

- A dashboard inbox listing open workflow tasks with document_id, task type, task status, creation time, and assignment.
- State badges reflecting the five workflow states (`ricevuto`, `in_elaborazione`, `da_validare`, `validato`, `eccezione`) consistently with backend enum values.
- Direct navigation from a task row to the document detail page via Next.js client-side routing (no full page reload).
- Retry action shown only for permitted exception tasks, with loading state feedback.
- Read state and list refresh without full page reload (via TanStack Query polling and localStorage persistence).
- All operator-visible labels and actions in Italian.

## Files Emitted

### Source (frontend)
- `src/node/frontend/app/workflow/inbox/page.tsx` — Next.js page for the task inbox route
- `src/node/frontend/app/workflow/inbox/components/TaskInbox.tsx` — Main inbox container component
- `src/node/frontend/app/workflow/inbox/components/TaskRow.tsx` — Single task row with actions (uses Next.js Link)
- `src/node/frontend/app/workflow/inbox/components/StateBadge.tsx` — Workflow state badge component
- `src/node/frontend/app/workflow/inbox/hooks/useTasks.ts` — TanStack Query hook for task list with polling
- `src/node/frontend/app/workflow/inbox/hooks/useRetryTask.ts` — Mutation hook for retry action
- `src/node/frontend/app/workflow/inbox/hooks/useReadState.ts` — Read state hook with localStorage persistence
- `src/node/frontend/app/workflow/inbox/types.ts` — TypeScript types for task DTOs
- `src/node/frontend/app/workflow/inbox/api.ts` — API client functions for tasks

### Tests
- `test/node/workflow-inbox/TaskInbox.test.tsx` — Component tests for inbox list, badges, retry visibility, refresh, read state
- `test/node/workflow-inbox/StateBadge.test.tsx` — Badge rendering for all five states
- `test/node/workflow-inbox/setup.ts` — Test setup with MSW handlers and fetch counter

### Docs & CI
- `docs/README_REQ-011.md` — This file
- `docs/KIT_REQ-011.md` — Iteration summary
- `ci/LTC.json` — Local test contract
- `ci/HOWTO.md` — Execution guide

## Dependencies

### Runtime
- `next` 14.x
- `react` 18.x
- `@tanstack/react-query` 5.x
- `tailwindcss` 3.x
- `lucide-react` (icons)

### Dev / Test
- `vitest` 1.x
- `@testing-library/react` 14.x
- `@testing-library/jest-dom` 6.x
- `jsdom`
- `msw` 2.x (Mock Service Worker for API mocking in tests)

### Upstream REQs
- **REQ-002/REQ-004** — shared frontend auth context (`useAuthUser` hook, `AuthUser` interface)
- **REQ-004** — shared API client, UI primitives
- **REQ-007** — document detail query (navigation target from task row)
- **REQ-010** — backend task and notification endpoints (API contract)

## Runtime Profiles

### Local
- API calls mocked via MSW in tests; for manual dev, a local FastAPI backend on `http://localhost:8000` or MSW browser integration.
- No external AWS dependencies required for local development or testing.

### AWS (target)
- Same Next.js build deployed to ECS Fargate.
- API base URL configured via `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL` environment variable pointing to the FastAPI service.
- Auth token obtained from OIDC/SAML IdP and passed as `Authorization: Bearer` header.

## Promotion Notes

### Auth context
`page.tsx` contains a `useAuthUser()` placeholder function that returns a hardcoded user. At promotion time, replace with the shared auth hook from `frontend.app.shared.auth` (REQ-002/REQ-004). The `AuthUser` interface is already aligned.

### QueryClient provider
`page.tsx` instantiates a local `QueryClient`. At promotion time, lift this to a shared layout provider from `frontend.app.shared.query` and remove the local instance.

### Next.js Link
`TaskRow.tsx` uses `next/link` for client-side navigation. Tests mock `next/link` as a plain `<a>` tag for jsdom compatibility.

## Pre Requirements Setup

1. Node.js 20.x installed
2. `npm ci` in the frontend workspace root
3. For manual dev: backend running locally (REQ-010 provides task endpoints)

## Local Development Mode

```bash
# Install dependencies
npm ci

# Run unit/component tests
npx vitest run --reporter=verbose

# Run in dev mode (requires backend)
npm run dev
```

## Target Runtime Mode

```bash
# Build for production
npm run build

# Environment variables for AWS deployment
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://api.zen2030.example.com
```

The same components, hooks, types, and API client are used in both modes. Only the API base URL and auth token source differ.

## How to Run & Environment Setup

```bash
cd runs/kit/REQ-011

# Install
